item.content:encoded:
**Claude Code generates hardcoded hex colors by default because it has no way to know your token names — a `tokens.css` file and four lines in `CLAUDE.md` fix that.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Create a two-layer CSS custom property file, wire it into `CLAUDE.md`, and verify that every new component Claude Code generates uses `var(--color-primary)` and switches between light and dark with a single attribute flip on `<html>`.

**Three takeaways:**
- Two layers: *primitives* (`--blue-600: #2563eb`) and *semantic tokens* (`--color-primary: var(--blue-600)`). Components reference only the semantic layer, so dark mode overrides live in one place.
- Light defaults on `:root`; dark overrides under `[data-theme="dark"]` — `document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark')` switches the whole app.
- Four lines in `CLAUDE.md` tell Claude which file to reference and block hardcoded hex before it's written.

**Walk-through:**

**1. Create `src/styles/tokens.css`:**

```css
/* Primitives — raw values, never used directly in component CSS */
:root {
  --blue-600: #2563eb;
  --blue-400: #60a5fa;
  --grey-900: #111827;
  --grey-100: #f3f4f6;
  --white: #ffffff;
}

/* Semantic tokens — light defaults */
:root {
  --color-primary: var(--blue-600);
  --color-surface: var(--white);
  --color-text: var(--grey-900);
  --color-border: var(--grey-100);
}

/* Dark overrides — semantic layer only */
[data-theme="dark"] {
  --color-primary: var(--blue-400);
  --color-surface: var(--grey-900);
  --color-text: var(--grey-100);
  --color-border: #374151;
}
```

**2. Add to `CLAUDE.md`:**

```markdown
## Styling rules
- Use CSS custom properties from `src/styles/tokens.css` for all colors.
- Reference semantic tokens only: `--color-primary`, `--color-surface`,
  `--color-text`, `--color-border`.
- Never hardcode hex values or RGB in component CSS or JSX `style` props.
```

**3. Toggle dark mode in JS:**

```js
document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark')
```

**4. Verify:** Ask Claude Code to generate a new button component. It should emit `color: var(--color-text)`, not `color: #111827`. If it still writes hex, add a one-line note: "If you are about to write a hex color in CSS, stop and use a token instead."

**Limits:** Existing components with hardcoded colors need a one-time migration. Claude Code won't auto-lint for violations — pair this setup with a [stylelint `color-no-hex` rule](https://stylelint.io/user-guide/rules/color-no-hex/) or a PostToolUse hook that runs stylelint on every `.css` write. The two-layer approach also requires consistent naming across your team; a stray `--primary-btn-bg: #2563eb` hardcoded in a component bypasses the dark override entirely.

**Sources:** [GitLab design tokens implementation](https://design.gitlab.com/product-foundations/design-tokens-technical-implementation) · [MDN CSS custom properties](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_cascading_variables/Using_CSS_custom_properties) · [CSS architecture at scale — tokens and custom properties](https://dev.to/sabrielagency/css-architecture-that-actually-scales-design-tokens-custom-properties-and-the-systems-that-hold-22n3)

**A `PostToolUse` hook in `.claude/settings.json` with a `Write|Edit` matcher runs your formatter automatically after every Claude Code edit — no manual step, no style drift accumulating across a session.**

Claude Code writes syntactically correct code but doesn't know about your project's formatter. After a session touching 20 files, a batch `prettier --write .` at commit time rewrites the diff in ways that mix real changes with noise, obscuring what actually moved.

A `PostToolUse` hook fixes this at the source. Add to `.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [{
      "matcher": "Write|Edit",
      "hooks": [{
        "type": "command",
        "command": "bash .claude/hooks/autoformat.sh"
      }]
    }]
  }
}
```

The script reads the edited file path from stdin (Claude Code passes the full tool JSON there) and dispatches by extension:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
FILE=$(jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // .tool_input.path // empty' 2>/dev/null)
[[ -z "$FILE" ]] && exit 0

case "$FILE" in
  *.ts|*.tsx|*.js|*.jsx|*.json|*.css)
    npx prettier --write "$FILE" 2>/dev/null || true ;;
  *.py)
    ruff format "$FILE" 2>/dev/null || true ;;
  *.go)
    gofmt -w "$FILE" 2>/dev/null || true ;;
esac
```

The `|| true` on every branch is deliberate. A `PostToolUse` hook can't block or revert the edit — it already ran. A non-zero exit sends formatter error output into Claude's context, which turns a missing `.prettierrc` into a stream of complaints on every edit. Silencing formatter failures keeps the loop clean; real style issues will surface in CI.

Supabase's `studio` repo committed exactly this pattern — a `format_and_lint.sh` hook that reads stdin with `jq`, runs Prettier, then `eslint --fix` on TypeScript files. Their implementation is a useful reference for the stdin parsing and multi-tool chaining.

**What doesn't work here:** `PostToolUse` hooks run synchronously, so budget around 5 seconds. Running `eslint .` (the whole project) instead of `eslint "$FILE"` (only the changed file) will visibly slow every single edit. Target only the changed file. If you need to veto a write before it happens — not just clean it up after — that's a `PreToolUse` hook, not a `PostToolUse` one.

**Sources:** [Claude Code hooks — docs.anthropic.com](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks) · [Practical hook configs — morphllm.com](https://www.morphllm.com/claude-code-hooks-examples) · [Supabase hooks commit — git.adamlamers.com](https://git.adamlamers.com/PublicArchive/supabase/commit/48e0b0c5592ffa80a6b95849430be2233d36120b)

**Your Claude app is responding to "describe this image" with text that shows it never saw the image. Add an image source block to the content array and Claude analyses the actual pixels.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** Send any image — a screenshot, diagram, chart, or document page — directly to the Claude Messages API from a URL, a local file, or the Files API for reuse.

- Three source types cover every case: **URL** (`{"type":"url","url":"…"}`) for images already on the web with no authentication required; **base64** for local files or dynamically generated images; **file_id** via the Files API when the same image appears across multiple turns or requests — upload once, reference many times
- Limits that bite: URL source must be a public URL (basic auth, signed URLs, and redirect chains often fail silently); base64 images must fit in the request body (practical limit ~5 MB before encoding overhead); total images per request are capped at 20 standard or up to 100 with long-context; Claude can't read PDFs via the image API — use the document source type for PDF pages
- Image tokens count against your context window — a 1568×1568 image costs ~1600 tokens; resize large screenshots to 1568 px on the long edge before sending to keep inference costs predictable

**Walk-through:**

```python
import anthropic, base64, pathlib

client = anthropic.Anthropic()

# 1. URL source — fastest path for images already on the web
response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=1024,
    messages=[{
        "role": "user",
        "content": [
            {
                "type": "image",
                "source": {
                    "type": "url",
                    "url": "https://example.com/architecture-diagram.png"
                }
            },
            {"type": "text", "text": "List any single points of failure you see."}
        ]
    }]
)
print(response.content[0].text)

# 2. Base64 source — for local files and generated images
img_bytes = pathlib.Path("screenshot.png").read_bytes()
img_b64   = base64.standard_b64encode(img_bytes).decode()

response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=1024,
    messages=[{
        "role": "user",
        "content": [
            {
                "type": "image",
                "source": {
                    "type": "base64",
                    "media_type": "image/png",
                    "data": img_b64
                }
            },
            {"type": "text", "text": "List accessibility issues you see."}
        ]
    }]
)

# 3. Files API — upload once, reuse across requests
with open("company-logo.png", "rb") as f:
    file_obj = client.beta.files.upload(
        file=("company-logo.png", f, "image/png")
    )

# reference by file_id — no repeated base64 overhead
response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=512,
    messages=[{
        "role": "user",
        "content": [
            {
                "type": "image",
                "source": {"type": "file", "file_id": file_obj.id}
            },
            {"type": "text", "text": "Does this logo read clearly at 32×32 px?"}
        ]
    }]
)
```

**The limits that matter:**

- Supported formats: JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP — not SVG, not TIFF
- Max image size: 8000×8000 pixels or 20 MB (5 MB on Bedrock)
- URL source fails silently when the server returns a redirect, an auth challenge, or a non-image content-type — use base64 when you aren't certain the URL is stable and public
- The Files API (`client.beta.files`) is in beta; the `betas=["files-api-2025-04-14"]` header is required for the upload call but not for the reference

**Sources:** [Claude Vision documentation](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/vision) · [Files API reference](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/files)

**Postgres already stores your application data — pgvector adds a `vector` column type so semantic similarity search runs in the same database, no separate vector store required.**
**What you will be able to do after this:** Add embedding-based similarity search to a Supabase project without leaving Postgres, query with cosine distance, and build RAG pipelines where knowledge-base embeddings sit next to the structured data they describe.
**Three things to know before you start:** - pgvector stores dense vectors (up to 16,000 dimensions) in ordinary Postgres rows. Metadata filters (date, author, document type) run as regular SQL `WHERE` clauses alongside or before the vector scan. - The `<=>` operator returns **cosine distance** (0 = identical, 2 = maximally different) — *lower is closer*. Wrapping the query as `1 - (embedding <=> query_vec)` gives you cosine *similarity* (1 = identical), which reads more naturally. - Below roughly 100K rows, a sequential scan outperforms an IVFFLAT index because the overhead of maintaining and reading the index structure exceeds the scan cost. Add the index only after you have enough rows to measure the difference.
**Setup in Supabase (five SQL steps):** ```sql -- 1. Enable the extension (do this once per project) create extension if not exists vector;
-- 2. Add an embedding column to your documents table create table documents ( id bigserial primary key, content text, embedding vector(1536) -- match your embedding model's output size );
-- 3. Insert a document with its embedding (from your application layer) insert into documents (content, embedding) values ('Your document text here', '[0.123, -0.456, ...]');
-- 4. Find the 5 closest documents to a query embedding select id, content, 1 - (embedding <=> '[0.1, 0.2, ...]'::vector) as similarity from documents order by embedding <=> '[0.1, 0.2, ...]'::vector limit 5;
-- 5. Add an IVFFlat index once you have >100K rows create index on documents using ivfflat (embedding vector_cosine_ops) with (lists = 100); ```
From your Python app, generate the embedding with `openai.embeddings.create()` or `sentence-transformers`, serialize it as a list, and pass it as a parameter. Supabase's `vecs` Python client wraps this in a collection API that handles table creation and query syntax for you.
**Sources:** [pgvector: Embeddings and vector similarity — Supabase docs](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/extensions/pgvector) · [Build Semantic Search with Supabase, pgvector & OpenAI Embeddings — YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=31LWbtCa70U) · [vecs Python client — Supabase docs](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/ai/vecs-python-client)

**Every subagent in a workflow inherits the session's effort level by default — set `effort:` per-call to run lookups cheap and reserve the reasoning budget for synthesis steps.**

When you fan out subagents in a Claude Code workflow, they all inherit the session's effort level. If your session runs at `high`, the agent doing a simple schema check burns the same compute budget as the one writing an architectural analysis. Claude Code v2.1.292 added per-call control.

**The resolution order** (highest precedence wins):
1. `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` env var — session-wide override
2. `effort:` on the `Agent` tool call — per-call override
3. Agent definition's `effort:` field in `.claude/agents/<name>.md` frontmatter — per-type default
4. Session effort level — the fallback

**In practice** — a two-phase pipeline with differentiated effort:

```javascript
// Phase 1: cheap parallel checks
const verdicts = await parallel(
  candidates.map(c => () =>
    agent(`Does ${c.name} match the schema? Return {match: bool}`, {
      effort: "low",   // boolean check — no nuanced reasoning needed
      schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA,
    })
  )
)

const matches = candidates.filter((_, i) => verdicts[i].match)

// Phase 2: expensive synthesis only on what passed
const report = await agent(
  `Summarise the ${matches.length} matching candidates and recommend next steps`,
  { effort: "high" }   // full reasoning budget for the decision output
)
```

**For CI pipelines**, set `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=medium` in the environment to cap costs across the whole run. Accept that very complex tasks may occasionally need a manual rerun at higher effort.

**The limit:** `effort: "low"` cuts the reasoning budget and can miss edge cases or subtleties a `medium` or `high` run would catch. Use it only for tasks with a clear correct/incorrect output — format validation, schema checks, simple lookups. Tasks requiring judgment about ambiguous inputs should stay at `medium` or higher.

**Sources:** [Claude Code Agent tool reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/agents) · [Subagent effort control — community guide](https://wmedia.es/es/tips/claude-code-subagentes-effort-bajo) · [Claude Code v2.1.292 release notes](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/claude-code-21302-turns-on-fork-subagents-in-sdk-and-apps)

**What you'll be able to do after this:** run automated accessibility checks on every AI-generated component as part of your existing Playwright suite, with the violated rule and failing element named in the test output.

AI-generated components are produced by sampling from training data — they write code that looks like UI, but they have no rendering feedback loop. Common outputs: buttons with no accessible label, form fields missing `<label>` associations, color combinations that fail WCAG AA contrast, and interactive elements that never show focus styles. A visual review in the design tool or Claude Code misses most of these.

**Three takeaways:**
- `@axe-core/playwright` is a three-line addition to any existing Playwright test that asserts zero accessibility violations on a rendered page
- When it fails, it names the violated rule and each failing element — enough to fix or regenerate the component before code review
- It is a static DOM scan: it catches rule violations but not all usability issues; run it alongside, not instead of, keyboard and screen reader testing

**Walk-through**

Install:

```bash
npm install --save-dev @axe-core/playwright
```

Add a scan to any component page test:

```typescript
import { checkA11y } from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('generated card passes accessibility', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/components/card');
  await checkA11y(page);
});
```

`checkA11y` throws a formatted assertion error if axe finds violations — the test output names each rule ID and failing element. For a suite of generated components:

```typescript
const components = ['card', 'modal', 'dropdown', 'form-field'];

for (const name of components) {
  test(`${name} passes axe scan`, async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto(`/components/${name}`);
    await checkA11y(page);
  });
}
```

**Most common violations in AI-generated UI** — check these first when the scan fails:
- `button-name`: icon-only `<button>` with no `aria-label`
- `label`: `<input>` or `<select>` with no associated `<label>`
- `color-contrast`: background/foreground pair failing WCAG AA (4.5:1 for normal text)
- `focus-visible`: `:focus` suppressed with `outline: none` and no replacement

Wire this into your Claude Code handoff routine: after landing a generated component, add it to `a11y.spec.ts` before marking the task done. CI catches any violation on the next push.

**Sources:** [Accessibility testing — playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing) · [`@axe-core/playwright` — github.com/dequelabs/axe-playwright](https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-playwright)

**Two hook fields that change blocking from a turn-ender into a feedback signal: `continueOnBlock: true` lets Claude recover from a rejected tool call; `updatedInput` silently rewrites tool inputs before they execute.**

A PreToolUse or PostToolUse hook that returns `{ok: false, reason: "..."}` ends the turn by default — Claude gets no information about what went wrong. `continueOnBlock: true` on the hook changes this: the rejection reason is fed back to Claude as a tool error, and the turn continues. Claude can then try a different path, ask a clarifying question, or adjust its approach.

```json
{
  "PreToolUse": [{
    "matcher": "Bash",
    "hooks": [{
      "type": "command",
      "command": "check-risky-commands.sh",
      "continueOnBlock": true
    }]
  }]
}
```

The shell script exits with code 2 (block) and prints a reason if the command matches a risky pattern. Claude sees the reason as a tool error and can adapt — without the user having to restart the turn.

**`updatedInput`** rewrites a tool's inputs before execution. Useful for silently redirecting writes to a sandbox:

```json
{
  "PreToolUse": [{
    "matcher": "Write",
    "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "redirect-to-sandbox.sh"}]
  }]
}
```

The script echoes `{"updatedInput": {"file_path": "/sandbox/myfile.txt", "content": "..."}}`. Claude Code replaces the original input with the rewritten one and calls the tool with the new values.

**`updatedToolOutput`** does the same for PostToolUse: replace what Claude sees after the tool ran — useful for stripping secrets from shell output before they enter Claude's context.

**Limits:**

- `updatedInput` replaces the *entire* input object — you must echo back every field, not just the ones you changed. Omit a field and it disappears from the tool call.
- Having multiple PreToolUse hooks rewrite the same tool's input is undefined behavior — only one rewrite wins, and the docs do not specify which. Chain multiple checks in one script instead.
- `continueOnBlock` applies to `command` and `http` hook types on PreToolUse and PostToolUse; agent hooks do not support it.

**Sources:** [Automate actions with hooks — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks-guide) · [Hooks reference — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)

**Every parallel subagent in a Claude Code SDK workflow inherits your session's model and runs until done — two parameters add hard cost controls without touching your orchestration logic.**

When a workflow fans out to 20 subagents, each one doing research or summarization at Sonnet rates, the bill is proportional to all of them finishing. Without explicit caps, a single misbehaving subagent can loop; without model routing, a subagent that just needs to classify "yes or no" pays the same rate as one reasoning through a complex migration.

```typescript
const results = await parallel(
  tasks.map(t => () =>
    agent(t.prompt, {
      max_budget_usd: 0.05,                                       // hard stop per subagent
      model: t.complex ? "claude-sonnet-4-6" : "claude-haiku-5-5",
      label: `task:${t.id}`,
    })
  )
)
```

**`max_budget_usd`**: The SDK tracks input + output token cost and terminates the subagent when the cap is reached, returning whatever it has accumulated. It is a hard stop, not a warning. Size conservatively for extraction or formatting tasks (these are predictable), more generously for reasoning or research (these are not). A subagent that hits its cap mid-task returns a partial result — that outcome should be handled by the caller.

**Model routing**: `claude-haiku-5-5` runs at roughly 1/15th the per-token cost of Sonnet, per Anthropic's published pricing. For classification, extraction, summarization, or boolean checks, Haiku delivers comparable output quality. Pass `model` per subagent based on task complexity rather than one model across the entire workflow.

**Permission prompt multiplication**: A fan-out of 20 subagents each needing to run a shell command prompts 20 times. Pre-approve read-only Bash operations with a `PreToolUse` hook so the workflow runs unattended:

```json
// .claude/settings.json — allow read-only shell ops inside subagents
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [{
      "matcher": "Bash",
      "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "bash .claude/hooks/auto-approve-reads.sh"}]
    }]
  }
}
```

The hook script exits 0 (allow) for read-only commands and exits 1 (block + prompt) for anything else.

One real limit: the spend cap does not distinguish between per-token cost and per-API-call overhead. A subagent that makes many short calls can exhaust its cap faster than one making fewer long calls. Calibrate caps against a sample run before setting them in production.

**Sources:** [Subagents in the Claude Code SDK](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents), [Hooks](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/hooks), [Effective harnesses for long-running agents](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents)

**When you call `query()` in the Claude Agent SDK, there is no spending cap by default — a stuck or looping run can consume far more than you intended. `ClaudeCodeOptions(max_budget_usd=0.50)` ends the run cleanly once estimated spend exceeds that, and `total_cost_usd` is always in the result regardless of how the run ended.**

Most programmatic agent loops set `max_turns` and assume that's enough protection. It isn't — a run with a large context window and many tool calls per turn can be expensive before it hits the turn limit. `max_budget_usd` adds a second, dollar-denominated brake.

**Python**

```python
from claude_code_sdk import query, ClaudeCodeOptions

options = ClaudeCodeOptions(
    max_budget_usd=0.50,   # stop once estimated spend exceeds $0.50
    max_turns=40,          # second guard: hard turn limit
)

messages = []
async for message in query(
    prompt="Refactor the auth module and add tests.",
    options=options,
):
    messages.append(message)

last = messages[-1]
if last.type == "result":
    print(f"Spent: ${last.total_cost_usd:.3f}")
    if last.subtype == "error_max_budget_usd":
        print("Budget cap hit — run aborted; partial work may still be useful")
    else:
        print("Completed normally")
```

**TypeScript**

```typescript
import { query, ClaudeCodeOptions } from "@anthropic-ai/claude-code";

const options: ClaudeCodeOptions = {
  maxBudgetUsd: 0.50,
  maxTurns: 40,
};

for await (const message of query({ prompt: "Refactor the auth module.", options })) {
  if (message.type === "result") {
    console.log(`Spent: $${message.total_cost_usd.toFixed(3)}`);
    if (message.subtype === "error_max_budget_usd") {
      console.log("Budget cap hit");
    }
  }
}
```

**Limits**

- The cap is an **estimate**, not a hard accounting guarantee — the actual charge may differ slightly.
- `max_budget_usd` is **per `query()` call**, not a session or account budget.
- Combine with `max_turns` — a run that hits the turn limit before the budget cap is a normal `error_max_turns` result; check `total_cost_usd` either way.
- Added in Python SDK v0.1.6 (`max_budget_usd`) and TypeScript SDK v0.1.6 (`maxBudgetUsd`).

**Sources:** [Claude Agent SDK — agent loop — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/agent-loop) · [Claude Code SDK examples — joshthomas.dev](https://git.joshthomas.dev/joshthomas/claude-code-sdk-example) · [Claude Code SDK cost control — towardsai.net](https://pub.towardsai.net/claude-code-sdk-cost-control-2026)

**Agent SDK v0.3.301 added `permission_policy` to individual MCP tool entries — set `always_ask` or `always_deny` on specific tools in an SDK-type MCP server config, so one dangerous tool doesn't inherit the server's blanket trust.**

Before this, per-server granularity meant the `allowedTools` list or a full `bypassPermissions` flag — either trust the whole server or block it. `permission_policy` adds per-tool control: flag high-impact tools for explicit approval while letting read-only tools run automatically.

**How to set it**

In an SDK-type MCP server definition, add `permission_policy` to each tool entry:

```typescript
const sdk = new ClaudeSDK({
  mcpServers: {
    myServer: {
      type: "sdk",
      tools: [
        {
          name: "read_file",
          // no permission_policy → inherits server default
        },
        {
          name: "write_file",
          permission_policy: "always_ask",   // prompt before every call
        },
        {
          name: "exec_command",
          permission_policy: "always_deny",  // blocked, no prompt
        },
      ],
    },
  },
});
```

`always_ask` prompts on every single invocation — use it for tools that write files, call external APIs, or have side effects you want to approve per-call. `always_deny` blocks the tool entirely, useful when you want most of a server's tools but need to exclude one destructive operation. `setMcpServers()` accepts the same structure for runtime updates.

**Limits**

- `permission_policy` applies only to SDK-type server configs, not to `stdio` or `http` transport types added in Claude Code settings.
- Field names come from third-party changelog sources; verify against the [official Agent SDK MCP docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp) before deploying.
- You must list each tool explicitly — there is no server-wide default policy that `permission_policy` overrides per-tool.

**Sources:** [Claude Agent SDK MCP docs — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp) · [SDK changelog Oct 2026 — gradually.ai](https://www.gradually.ai/en/changelogs/claude-agent-sdk/) · [Claude Code MCP permissions 2026 — techtaek.com](https://techtaek.com/claude-code-mcp-permissions-in-2026-when-to-use-allowedtools-instead-of-bypass-mode/)

**What you'll be able to do after this:** add responsive layout checks for your AI-generated components to an existing Playwright suite, catch horizontal overflow at three viewport widths in CI, and handle the multi-variant DOM pattern AI-generated components tend to produce.

**Three-bullet takeaway:**
- A single `projects` array in `playwright.config.ts` runs every spec at mobile (375px), tablet (768px), and desktop (1280px) — no per-test code changes
- `document.body.scrollWidth > window.innerWidth` catches text, images, and absolutely positioned elements that bleed past the viewport
- AI-generated components often render multiple breakpoint variants to the DOM simultaneously and use CSS to hide inactive ones — use `.locator(':visible')` to avoid matching hidden variants

---

## Step 1 — Three projects in one config

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  projects: [
    { name: 'mobile',  use: { viewport: { width: 375,  height: 812  } } },
    { name: 'tablet',  use: { viewport: { width: 768,  height: 1024 } } },
    { name: 'desktop', use: { viewport: { width: 1280, height: 900  } } },
  ],
  testDir: './tests',
});
```

Every spec file now runs three times, once per project. No test changes required.

## Step 2 — The overflow check

```typescript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('no horizontal overflow', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/components/card');
  const hasOverflow = await page.evaluate(
    () => document.body.scrollWidth > window.innerWidth
  );
  expect(hasOverflow).toBe(false);
});
```

Run this at each breakpoint to catch any content bleeding past the viewport edge.

## Step 3 — Handle the AI-generated component caveat

Many AI-generated components (and many design-system components) render all breakpoint variants to the DOM simultaneously and use CSS (`display: none`, `visibility: hidden`) to show the right one. A locator like `page.getByRole('heading')` may match the hidden mobile variant while running on desktop. Scope to visible elements:

```typescript
const heading = page
  .getByRole('heading', { name: 'Card title' })
  .locator(':visible')
  .first();
```

## Step 4 — Screenshot baseline

```typescript
await page.screenshot({
  path: `snapshots/card-${test.info().project.name}.png`,
  fullPage: true,
});
```

Run `playwright test --update-snapshots` once to create the baseline; subsequent CI runs catch visual regressions per breakpoint.

## Limits

- `scrollWidth > innerWidth` passes when an element uses `overflow: hidden` — content hidden inside a container won't be detected. For deeper coverage, walk the DOM checking `getComputedStyle(el).overflow !== 'hidden'` on each child.
- `setViewportSize()` alone does not emulate touch events or device pixel ratio; import `devices['iPhone 12']` from `@playwright/test` for full device emulation including touch handling.
- Screenshot comparisons need a committed baseline; the first run always passes. Regenerate snapshots in version control when layouts change intentionally.

**Sources:** [Playwright Emulation — Viewport — playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev/docs/emulation#viewport) · [Zero overflow in 10 minutes: responsive testing with Claude Code and Playwright — web-developpeur.com](https://www.web-developpeur.com/en/blog/responsive-testing-claude-code-playwright) · [Responsive overflow tests scaffold — github.com/CyberPunkCodes](https://github.com/CyberPunkCodes/responsive-overflow-tests)
